! A bimetal strip bending under a constant dT = 100, through ElasticSolve. This is
! the sibling ThermalBiMetal's case, mesh, materials and REFERENCE NORM, with the
! solver swapped, and the reason for having it is what ElasticSolve used to do with
! it: nothing at all.
!
! THE CAPABILITY WAS SILENTLY ABSENT. ElasticSolve read "Heat Expansion
! Coefficient" and "Reference Temperature" from the material, gathered the
! temperature difference per element, passed both into its assembly routine -- and
! the assembly never read either argument. Thermal strain was dropped, with no
! warning and no unused-keyword complaint, and the answer that came back was a
! converged zero. No test in the tree ran ElasticSolve with a heat expansion
! coefficient, which is why nothing said so.
!
! Run with the coefficients zeroed, this case returns a displacement norm of
! EXACTLY zero against a reference of 1.4e-3 -- the whole answer is the thermal
! strain -- so the test detects the old behaviour completely rather than by a
! margin.
!
! HOW IT IS IMPLEMENTED, and why it is not a term of its own. alpha * dT is an
! eigenstrain, so it enters through the affine offset channel that "Strain Load"
! uses (see fem/tests/ElasticStressLoad): sigma = C : ( eps - eps0 ), with the
! thermal part added to eps0 and C : eps0 contracted THROUGH the constitutive
! model. StressSolve keeps its thermal term and its "Strain Load" term apart, in
! different lines of its force vector; they are one capability, and unifying them
! is what made the second one arrive with the first.
!
! Also fixed here rather than separately: the reported STRAIN. StressSolve reports
! the elastic strain, with the thermal part subtracted, and the stress that follows
! from it. ElasticSolve's postprocessing did neither, so had the assembly alone
! been fixed the fields would have disagreed even though the displacement matched.
!
! The material is deliberately the sibling's: two bodies whose expansion
! coefficients differ by a factor of two, which is what makes the strip bend rather
! than merely grow, and the bending is what a dropped or mis-signed eigenstrain
! cannot fake. Plane strain, small deflection, direct solve.

Check Keywords Warn

Header
  Mesh DB "." "beams"
End

Simulation
  Max Output Level = 5
  Coordinate System = Cartesian
  Simulation Type = Steady State
  Steady State Max Iterations = 1
End

Constants
  Permittivity Of Vacuum = 8.8542e-12
End

Body 1
  Name = "metal1"
  Equation = 1
  Material = 1
  Initial Condition = 1
End

Body 2
  Name = "metal2"
  Equation = 1
  Material = 2
  Initial Condition = 1
End

Initial Condition 1
  Temperature = 400.0
End

Equation 1
  Active Solvers = 1
  Plane Stress = FALSE
End

Solver 1
  Equation = "LinearDisp"
  Procedure = "ElasticSolve" "ElasticSolver"
  Variable = "Displacement"
  Variable DOFs = Integer 2

  ! Small strain, so the kinematics are StressSolve's and the reference norm below
  ! is comparable digit for digit.
  Large Deflection = False

  ! The scalar the thermal strain is measured against. Present where Displacement
  ! is, and initialised above to something other than the reference temperature.
  Exported Variable 1 = Temperature

  Linear System Solver = Direct
  Linear System Direct Method = UMFPACK

  Nonlinear System Convergence Tolerance = Real 1.0e-7
  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = Integer 1
  Nonlinear System Relaxation Factor = Real 1

  Steady State Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-6
  Optimize Bandwidth = True
End

Material 1
  Density = Real 1
  Youngs Modulus = 1e9
  Poisson Ratio = Real 0.3
  Reference Temperature = 300.0
  Heat Expansion Coefficient = 1.0e-4
End

Material 2
  Density = Real 1
  Youngs Modulus = 1e9
  Poisson Ratio = Real 0.3
  Reference Temperature = 300.0
  Heat Expansion Coefficient = 2.0e-4
End

Boundary Condition 1
  Name = "beam_fixed"
  Target Boundaries(2) = 4 7
  Displacement 1 = 0.0
  Displacement 2 = 0.0
End

! StressSolve's own published value for this case, unchanged. ElasticSolve
! reproduces it to 6.5e-12 relative.
Solver 1 :: Reference Norm = Real 1.39759434511988389E-003
RUN
